-material: pip install -r\n+# docs/site/requirements.txt) and runs the docs drift test (part of `make test`).\n+docs-check:\n+\tmkdocs build --strict -d /tmp/ard-site\n+\tgo test ./internal/docscheck/...\n+\n clean:\n \trm -rf bin\ndiff --git a/README.md b/README.md\nindex 7c170c8..cdaf9c9 100644\n--- a/README.md\n+++ b/README.md\n@@ -2,7 +2,9 @@\n \n ard makes a GitHub repository maintain itself. Scanners file deduplicated issues, triage agents turn each issue into a plan, a worker agent implements it on a branch, reviewer agents critique the PR, and a merger lands it once every review passes.\n \n-> **Status:** design stage. Nothing runs yet. The design lives in [docs/proposal.md](docs/proposal.md), and the work is tracked as [GitHub issues](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues) grouped into phase milestones.\n+**Documentation:** <https://albertcbrown.github.io/ard/> (sources in [docs/site](docs/site)).\n+\n+> **Status:** under active development. The design lives in [docs/proposal.md](docs/proposal.md), and the work is tracked as [GitHub issues](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues) grouped into phase milestones.\n \n ## How it works\n \ndiff --git a/docs/layout.md b/docs/layout.md\nindex 8984b19..5446d64 100644\n--- a/docs/layout.md\n+++ b/docs/layout.md\n@@ -3,8 +3,11 @@\n Every top-level directory and Go package, with what it holds. Keep it current: a PR that adds, removes or renames a directory or package updates this file in the same PR.\n \n ```\n+mkdocs.yml                  MkDocs Material config for the user docs site\n+docs/site/                  user docs (published to GitHub Pages)\n cmd/ard/                    CLI entry point; wire a new subcommand by adding one line to handlers()\n internal/cli/               cobra command tree, global flags, slog logging; Handlers is the plug-in point\n+internal/docscheck/         checks docs/site against the CLI commands and config keys (drift test)\n internal/botcheck/          tests for the bot-commit audit in .github/workflows/bot-commits.yml\n internal/github/            GitHub client: domain types, Reader/Writer/Client, App auth, retry transport; query/ (search builder), fake/ (test double), clienttest/ (conformance suite)\n internal/config/            .ard/config.yaml + role directory loading into a Resolved value (pure, fs.FS in)\ndiff --git a/docs/proposal.md b/docs/proposal.md\nindex cec8c5c..581e501 100644\n--- a/docs/proposal.md\n+++ b/docs/proposal.md\n@@ -528,7 +528,7 @@ How ard itself is built, published and deployed. Jenkins and Argo CD do the work\n \n ## Rollout\n \n-Start on one repo (albertcbrown/ard itself, dogfooding; decided, #6) with the local executor and a human approval gate; humans merge until Gate 2, then the merger takes over. Widen only when the numbers say the loop is trustworthy. Gate 2 is run from [docs/runbooks/gate-2.md](runbooks/gate-2.md).\n+Start on one repo (albertcbrown/ard itself, dogfooding; decided, #6) with the local executor and a human approval gate; humans merge until Gate 2, then the merger takes over. Widen only when the numbers say the loop is trustworthy. Gate 2 is run from [docs/site/operations/gate-2-runbook.md](site/operations/gate-2-runbook.md).\n \n ```mermaid\n flowchart LR\ndiff --git a/docs/runbooks/pages-setup.md b/docs/runbooks/pages-setup.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..743e82c\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/docs/runbooks/pages-setup.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@\n+# Publishing the docs site (human steps)\n+\n+Agents cannot write under `.github/`, so a maintainer does these steps once. The site is MkDocs Material, built from `docs/site/` with `mkdocs.yml`.\n+\n+## 1. Add `.github/workflows/docs.yml`\n+\n+- **Triggers:** `push` to `main` and `pull_request`, both with `paths: [docs/site/**, mkdocs.yml, internal/config/**, internal/cli/**, internal/docscheck/**]`.\n+- **`build` job:** check out, set up Python and Go, `pip install -r docs/site/requirements.txt`, then `make docs-check` (`mkdocs build --strict` plus the Go drift test).\n+- **`deploy` job** (push to `main` only, `needs: build`): `actions/configure-pages`, build to `site/`, `actions/upload-pages-artifact`, `actions/deploy-pages`. Permissions: `contents: read`, `pages: write`, `id-token: write`. Environment `github-pages`.\n+\n+## 2. Enable Pages\n+\n+Settings, Pages, Source: **GitHub Actions**. The site appears at `https://albertcbrown.github.io/ard/`.\n+\n+## 3. Optional\n+\n+Add the docs build to the required checks. Because `docs/site/` is not a protected path, docs pull requests merge without a human unless you add it.\n+\n+## 4. Check by hand\n+\n+Follow Getting started on a scratch repository to a first triaged issue and note the result on the pull request.\ndiff --git a/docs/site/configuration.md b/docs/site/configuration.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..79c6fa1\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/docs/site/configuration.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,96 @@\n+# Configuration reference\n+\n+ard is configured by convention. A role exists because its directory exists, and `.ard/config.yaml` holds only global settings and overrides. Anything unset takes the default below. Check your file with `ard config check`; `ard serve` reads it from `origin/main`, so a change takes effect once it is merged. The full design is in the [proposal](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/docs/proposal.md).\n+\n+Durations are Go-style strings such as `30m` or `4h`; `d` is not accepted, so write `720h` for 30 days. No loop interval may be under `1m`.\n+\n+## Global keys\n+\n+| Key | Default | Meaning |\n+| --- | --- | --- |\n+| `max_pods` | `3` | Job slots shared by all loops |\n+| `executor` | `local` | Where jobs run |\n+| `agent` | `claude` | The agent CLI adapter |\n+| `require_approval` | `true` | A worker starts only after a human adds `ard:approved` |\n+| `budget_usd_per_day` | `50` | Daily dollar cap for the repository (UTC day) |\n+| `max_budget_usd` | `5` | Dollar cap for one job |\n+| `global_max_open` | `30` | Cap on open ard-filed issues across all scanners |\n+| `paused` | `false` | Kill switch: no new claims while true |\n+\n+## Loop intervals\n+\n+| Key | Default | Meaning |\n+| --- | --- | --- |\n+| `intervals.scanners` | `4h` | How often scanners run |\n+| `intervals.triage` | `15m` | Triage loop poll |\n+| `intervals.workers` | `5m` | Worker loop poll |\n+| `intervals.reviewers` | `5m` | Reviewer loop poll |\n+| `intervals.merger` | `5m` | Merger loop poll |\n+| `intervals.prwatch` | `5m` | PR watch loop poll (minimum `1m`): checks ard's open pull requests for human merges, closes without merge, long-pending checks and red drafts |\n+| `pr_watch.pending_timeout` | `2h` | How long a required check may stay pending before the PR watch flags it (at least the interval, at most `7d`) |\n+\n+The Reaper runs every minute and is not configurable.\n+\n+## Intake\n+\n+| Key | Default | Meaning |\n+| --- | --- | --- |\n+| `intake.mode` | `label` | `label`: only issues a maintainer labels enter triage. `all`: every human-filed issue enters |\n+| `intake.label` | `ard:pending` | The label that admits an issue in `label` mode. It may not be one of ard's own label names |\n+\n+## Merge gate\n+\n+| Key | Default | Meaning |\n+| --- | --- | --- |\n+| `merge.protected_paths` | none | A pull request touching any entry waits for human approval. There is no built-in list: removing an entry removes its gate. An entry ending in `/` is a directory prefix, anything else is a glob |\n+\n+`ard init` writes a recommended list that includes `.github/`, `.ard/config.yaml`, the role directories, `CODEOWNERS`, `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/`, `.mcp.json` and build and deploy files.\n+\n+## Stale scanner issues\n+\n+| Key | Default | Meaning |\n+| --- | --- | --- |\n+| `stale.inactive_for` | `720h` | A scanner issue untouched this long is commented on and closed |\n+| `stale.suppress_for` | `2160h` | How long the closed finding stays suppressed |\n+\n+## Redaction\n+\n+| Key | Default | Meaning |\n+| --- | --- | --- |\n+| `redact.gitleaks.enabled` | `false` | Also scan logs with gitleaks before they are committed |\n+| `redact.gitleaks.path` | `gitleaks` | The gitleaks binary |\n+| `redact.gitleaks.block_autoclose_when_missing` | `false` | Refuse to autoclose when gitleaks is enabled but missing |\n+\n+## Sidecars\n+\n+A sidecar is a read-only reference repository mounted for a role. `<name>` is yours to choose.\n+\n+| Key | Default | Meaning |\n+| --- | --- | --- |\n+| `sidecars.<name>.repo` | required | `owner/repo` of the sidecar |\n+| `sidecars.<name>.ref` | `main` | Branch, tag or commit to mount |\n+| `roles_repo` | none | Optional shared roles repository: `{repo: owner/name, ref: <branch, tag or commit>}`. Its role directories load beneath the repo's own `.ard/`, and a local role of the same name wins. With no `ref` it follows the shared repo's default branch |\n+\n+## Overrides\n+\n+`overrides` maps a role path (`scanners/cve`, `triage/lead`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`) to a mapping of the keys below. A role may also set `agent`, `max_budget_usd` and `budget_usd_per_day` to override the global values. An unknown key is an error.\n+\n+| Key | Default | Applies to | Meaning |\n+| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n+| `overrides` | none | all | The mapping itself |\n+| `every` | global `intervals.scanners` | scanners | Run interval for this scanner (an LLM scanner needs at least `24h`) |\n+| `max_open` | `5` | scanners | Open issues this scanner may have |\n+| `group_size` | `10` | scanners | Findings grouped into one issue |\n+| `autoclose` | `false` | scanners | Close the issue when the finding disappears (an error on an LLM scanner) |\n+| `route` | `pending` (executable) or `none` (LLM) | scanners | Where filed issues go: `pending` enters triage, `none` files with the scanner label only, `needs-human` files with `ard:needs-human` |\n+| `model` | adapter default | agents | Model name passed to the agent |\n+| `timeout` | `30m` | all roles | Job deadline |\n+| `sidecar` | none | agents | Name of a `sidecars` entry to mount |\n+| `after` | none | triage | Roles whose plan sections must exist first |\n+| `plan_section` | none | triage | The `plan.md` heading the role owns |\n+| `tests` | `make test` | workers | Command the worker runs before opening a PR |\n+| `max_test_fixes` | `2` | workers | Fix attempts after a red test run |\n+| `max_review_rounds` | `3` | workers | Review rounds before `needs-human` |\n+| `reviewers` | all reviewer roles | workers | Reviewer roles requested for this worker's PRs |\n+| `diff_warn_lines` | `2000` | workers | Changed lines above which the PR carries a large-change note |\n+| `diff_max_lines` | `3000` | workers | Changed lines above which the change is held for a human |\ndiff --git a/docs/site/getting-started.md b/docs/site/getting-started.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..e1b6378\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/docs/site/getting-started.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,67 @@\n+# Getting started\n+\n+This takes a repository from nothing to a first triaged issue. Run the `ard` commands from a clone of the target repository, or pass `--repo owner/name`.\n+\n+## 1. Create and install the GitHub App\n+\n+ard acts as a GitHub App, never as a personal token. Create an App you own and give it exactly these repository permissions:\n+\n+| Permission | Access |\n+| --- | --- |\n+| Contents | Read and write |\n+| Issues | Read and write |\n+| Pull requests | Read and write |\n+| Checks | Read and write |\n+| Commit statuses | Read |\n+| Metadata | Read |\n+\n+Do **not** grant `workflows`. Without it GitHub itself rejects any push that edits `.github/workflows/`. Install the App on the target repository, then note the App ID and installation ID and download the private key (a `.pem` file; keep it mode 0600).\n+\n+Give `ard serve` the App through its environment: `ARD_GITHUB_APP_ID`, `ARD_GITHUB_INSTALLATION_ID` and the key as `ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE` (a path) or `ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY`.\n+\n+## 2. Let the App push to main\n+\n+ard commits its own files under `.ard/<issue#>/` straight to `main`. If `main` has a ruleset, add the App to the ruleset's bypass list. Without that, pushes fail with a `GH013` ruleset rejection.\n+\n+## 3. Audit the bot's commits\n+\n+Set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the App's `<slug>[bot]` login, and add a workflow that fails when a bot commit changes anything outside a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. The example is [.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml). The App cannot write workflows, so you add this one yourself.\n+\n+## 4. Initialise the repository\n+\n+```sh\n+ard init\n+```\n+\n+`ard init` opens a pull request with the starter `.ard/` tree: `config.yaml` and the starter role directories. Review and merge it. Then:\n+\n+```sh\n+ard labels sync\n+ard config check\n+```\n+\n+`ard labels sync` creates the `ard:` labels and never deletes one. `ard config check` prints the resolved configuration and reports every problem with its file and key.\n+\n+## 5. Store the model credential\n+\n+Set `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` (a subscription token from `claude setup-token`) or `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in the environment of `ard serve`. By default the token wins when both are set; `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` set to `oauth` or `apikey` forces one. The value is passed to the agent job and is never logged. If you use a subscription, turn off extra usage in the account so a usage limit is not billed as overage.\n+\n+## 6. Start serving\n+\n+Try a dry run first. It logs every side effect without performing it:\n+\n+```sh\n+ard serve --dry-run\n+```\n+\n+Then start it for real:\n+\n+```sh\n+ard serve\n+```\n+\n+## 7. File and label a first issue\n+\n+File an issue describing a small change. With the default `intake.mode: label`, add the `ard:pending` label to admit it. Within one triage interval (15 minutes by default) the lead writes `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md` and the issue gains a triage-done label. If `require_approval` is on, add `ard:approved` to let a worker start.\n+\n+If nothing happens, see [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md).\ndiff --git a/docs/site/index.md b/docs/site/index.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..e443979\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/docs/site/index.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,41 @@\n+# ard\n+\n+ard makes a GitHub repository maintain itself. One `ard` process per repository runs a set of loops that share a small pool of job slots (`max_pods`, default 3). A deterministic controller owns every GitHub and git side effect. Agents only read and write files in a worktree.\n+\n+## The loop\n+\n+1. **Scanners** find problems (CVEs, lint, missing tests, docs gaps, secrets) and file deduplicated issues.\n+2. **Triage** roles (lead, engineer, and optionally product manager, architect, designer, documentation planner) read an issue and write `plan.md`.\n+3. **Workers** implement the plan on a `worker/...` branch, run the tests and open a draft pull request.\n+4. **Reviewers** (engineering, security, license, product) critique the pull request and ask for fixes.\n+5. **The merger** squash-merges a pull request once every review passed and its checks are green.\n+6. **The Reaper** marks dead or overdue jobs `ard:executor:failed`.\n+\n+`.ard/<issue#>/` in your repository holds each issue's plan, per-role summaries and full logs.\n+\n+## The `ard:` labels\n+\n+GitHub labels prefixed `ard:` are the queue and the state machine. Most follow `ard:<stage>:<role>[:<state>]`, for example `ard:triage:lead:requested`. Run `ard labels sync` to create them. The ones you will touch:\n+\n+| Label | Meaning |\n+| --- | --- |\n+| `ard:pending` | Ready for triage. A maintainer adds it in `label` intake mode |\n+| `ard:approved` | Human go-ahead for a worker to start |\n+| `ard:needs-approval` | The pull request touches a protected path and waits for a human |\n+| `ard:needs-human` | Blocked; automation stops until a human acts |\n+| `ard:hold` | The controller ignores this issue or pull request |\n+| `ard:executor:failed` | A job died or ran over time; remove the label to retry |\n+| `ard:tests:failed` | The worker could not get the tests green |\n+| `ard:keep` | Keep failed-job resources past 24 hours |\n+| `ard:pause` | On the status issue: stop all new claims |\n+\n+The full state machine is in the [design proposal](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/docs/proposal.md).\n+\n+## What humans still decide\n+\n+- **Approval.** With `require_approval` on (the default), a worker starts only after a human adds `ard:approved`.\n+- **Protected paths.** A pull request touching any `merge.protected_paths` entry (for example `.github/`) waits for a human approval.\n+- **Merges.** The merger lands pull requests that pass every gate. To keep merging by hand, start `ard serve` without the `merger` loop (see [Operations](operations.md)).\n+- **Workflows.** Agents cannot edit `.github/workflows/`; a person does.\n+\n+Next: [Install](install.md), then [Getting started](getting-started.md). The design source of truth is the [proposal](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/docs/proposal.md).\ndiff --git a/docs/site/install.md b/docs/site/install.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..ad0e818\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/docs/site/install.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,18 @@\n+# Install\n+\n+ard runs on macOS and Linux. It needs:\n+\n+- Go (to build), `make` and `git` 2.47.0 or newer. An older git fails some fetches.\n+- The `claude` CLI for the default agent.\n+- Docker, only for roles that ship a `Dockerfile` (the cve, docs and lint scanners).\n+\n+Build from source:\n+\n+```sh\n+git clone https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard\n+cd ard\n+make build\n+./bin/ard version\n+```\n+\n+Put `bin/ard` on your `PATH`. A Homebrew cask is planned (#177); this page will point to it once it lands.\ndiff --git a/docs/site/operations.md b/docs/site/operations.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..e8b61af\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/docs/site/operations.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,67 @@\n+# Operations\n+\n+## Running and stopping\n+\n+`ard serve` runs every loop. `ard serve --loops scanners,triage` runs only the named loops, which is how you keep merging by hand: leave out `merger`. A dry run (`ard serve --dry-run`) logs every side effect to a JSONL ledger instead of performing it.\n+\n+Send `SIGUSR1` to the process to make every loop run one tick now (waking loops, #161).\n+\n+## Kill switch\n+\n+Any one of these pauses all new claims (running jobs finish):\n+\n+- set `paused: true` in `config.yaml` on `main`;\n+- add the `ard:pause` label to the status issue.\n+\n+Remove it to resume. The costs loop keeps running while paused.\n+\n+## The status issue\n+\n+ard keeps one pinned status issue per repository. It shows what each loop last did, and it carries `ard:pause`.\n+\n+## Logs and state\n+\n+- **Logs.** Every agent run's full output is committed to `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<timestamp>.log`, after redaction. The controller logs to stderr as structured lines; `--log-level debug` shows more.\n+- **State directory.** `~/.ard/` on the host holds the config snapshots (`config/`), scanner state (`state/<owner>/<repo>/<scanner>.db`) and job worktrees. It is a cache: GitHub is the truth.\n+\n+## The Reaper and failure causes\n+\n+The Reaper runs every minute. It marks a job that died or ran over time `ard:executor:failed` with a cause, keeps its worktree and log for 24 hours (`ard:keep` extends that) and cleans up. Causes:\n+\n+| Cause | Meaning |\n+| --- | --- |\n+| `heartbeat_quiet` | No log output for 10 minutes |\n+| `deadline` | The job ran past its `timeout` |\n+| `controller_restart` | The job was orphaned across a restart |\n+| `no_process` | The job never started or its process vanished |\n+| `rate_limit` | The agent reported a transient API error; retried once automatically |\n+| `clone_failed` | Preparing the worktree failed at git or clone |\n+| `usage_limit` | The agent's usage limit is exhausted |\n+\n+To retry a failed job, remove `ard:executor:failed` from the issue.\n+\n+## Usage limits\n+\n+When an agent reports its usage limit is exhausted, ard records it and stops starting jobs for that agent until the limit resets, instead of failing each job. `budget_usd_per_day` and `max_budget_usd` cap spending in dollars.\n+\n+## The cost table\n+\n+ard writes a cost table into each issue body, between ard-owned markers, from recorded usage (#172). Do not edit it by hand; ard rewrites it.\n+\n+## Upgrades\n+\n+Build the new `ard`, stop `ard serve` and start the new binary. Run `ard config check` first, because a new release may reject a key the old one accepted. Review the release for changes to the starter roles; `ard init` proposes updates as a pull request and never deletes.\n+\n+## Backups and rebuilding\n+\n+Everything that matters is in GitHub (issues, labels, `.ard/`). `~/.ard/state/` can be deleted. To rebuild a scanner's state from its issues, stop `ard serve` and run:\n+\n+```sh\n+ard rebuild-state cve\n+```\n+\n+To rebuild a damaged clone, stop `ard serve`, delete the damaged clone under `~/.ard/`, and start `ard serve` again. Nothing in the clone is the only copy of anything.\n+\n+## Runbooks\n+\n+The [Gate 2 runbook](operations/gate-2-runbook.md) is the checklist used to prove the first loop on real issues.\ndiff --git a/docs/runbooks/gate-2.md b/docs/site/operations/gate-2-runbook.md\nsimilarity index 98%\nrename from docs/runbooks/gate-2.md\nrename to docs/site/operations/gate-2-runbook.md\nindex 975819b..1eaff10 100644\n--- a/docs/runbooks/gate-2.md\n+++ b/docs/site/operations/gate-2-runbook.md\n@@ -2,7 +2,7 @@\n \n Gate 2 proves triage, worker and review on real issues before the merger takes\n over. ard runs live with the local executor on the owner's Mac, and a human\n-merges every PR. See [docs/proposal.md: Rollout](../proposal.md#rollout).\n+merges every PR. See [docs/proposal.md: Rollout](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/docs/proposal.md#rollout).\n \n **Done when** (from #39):\n \ndiff --git a/docs/site/requirements.txt b/docs/site/requirements.txt\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..e95ede0\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/docs/site/requirements.txt\n@@ -0,0 +1,2 @@\n+mkdocs==1.6.1\n+mkdocs-material==9.5.50\ndiff --git a/docs/site/roles.md b/docs/site/roles.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..6b92464\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/docs/site/roles.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,53 @@\n+# Roles\n+\n+A role is a directory under `.ard/`. Its type is the parent directory, its name is the directory name, and `config.yaml` never lists it.\n+\n+| Directory | Type | What it does |\n+| --- | --- | --- |\n+| `.ard/scanners/<name>/` | scanner | Finds problems and files issues |\n+| `.ard/triage/<name>/` | triage | Plans an issue |\n+| `.ard/workers/<name>/` | worker | Implements a plan |\n+| `.ard/reviewers/<name>/` | reviewer | Reviews a pull request |\n+\n+A role is an agent when it has a `prompt.txt`, and an executable (for scanners) when it has a `scan` script. A role with a `Dockerfile` runs in Docker; one without runs as a host subprocess.\n+\n+## Starter roles\n+\n+`ard init` ships these:\n+\n+- **Scanners:** `cve`, `docs`, `error-codes`, `lint`, `missing-tests`, `trufflehog`, `trufflehog-history`.\n+- **Triage:** `lead` (routes the issue), `engineer` (writes the implementation plan), `product-manager`, `architect`, `designer`, `documentation-planner`.\n+- **Workers:** `engineer`.\n+- **Reviewers:** `eng-review`, `security-review`, `license-review`, `product-manager-review`.\n+\n+Role directories are protected paths in the recommended config, so changing one takes a human approval.\n+\n+## Writing a role prompt\n+\n+`prompt.txt` is the whole prompt. The controller gives the agent:\n+\n+- the issue and its comments as files, `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, which are untrusted text;\n+- earlier roles' output under `.ard/<issue#>/`;\n+- on a fix round, `test-output.log`;\n+- an optional read-only sidecar repository at `$ARD_SIDECAR`.\n+\n+Tell the agent to treat the input files as data and to write only its own output file. Say which status values it may report and what each means. Test a new prompt with `ard serve --dry-run` before relying on it.\n+\n+## The role file\n+\n+Each agent job ends by writing one file, `.ard/<issue#>/<role>.md` (workers write `worker-<role>.md`). It begins with frontmatter that code checks:\n+\n+- the first line is exactly `---`, and the frontmatter ends at the next line that is exactly `---`;\n+- `role` is the role's name; `status` is `done`, `needs-human` or `blocked` (reviewers use their own verdicts); `attempt` is a number; `next` is an optional list of roles to run next.\n+\n+Anything else in the frontmatter fails the job. The body, after the closing `---`, becomes the comment the controller posts.\n+\n+## The write allowlist\n+\n+An agent cannot push or edit GitHub. The controller commits a role's output only if every changed path is allowed:\n+\n+- every role may write its own role file;\n+- a triage role may also write its own `## <section>` of `plan.md` (the heading named by its `plan_section`); the designer may also write under `.ard/<issue#>/design/`;\n+- a worker may change code in its worktree, but never `.github/`, `.ard/` (other than its report), secret-shaped files, symlinks, binaries or files over 1 MiB, and never more changed lines than `diff_max_lines`.\n+\n+Any other change is rejected and the job ends `needs-human`. Nobody may change `issue.md`, another role's file or delete `plan.md`.\ndiff --git a/docs/site/security.md b/docs/site/security.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..746b7d5\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/docs/site/security.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@\n+# Security model\n+\n+Agents read text that strangers can write, so the controls are in code and permissions, not in prompts. The full reasoning is in the [proposal](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/docs/proposal.md).\n+\n+- **Agents hold no GitHub token.** Only the controller talks to GitHub and runs git side effects. An agent reads and writes files in a worktree, and the controller decides what to commit.\n+- **Untrusted input is passed as files.** Issue text and comments arrive as `.ard-input/<issue#>/` files that are never committed, not inside the prompt. Role prompts tell agents to treat them as data.\n+- **The GitHub App is narrow.** It lacks the `workflows` permission, so GitHub rejects any push that edits `.github/workflows/`. A guard also refuses all of `.github/`.\n+- **Protected paths.** A pull request touching `merge.protected_paths` needs a human approval.\n+- **Write allowlists.** A role's output is committed only if every path is allowed (see [Roles](roles.md)).\n+- **Log redaction.** Before a log is committed, close-out removes known secret values and replaces the host's own paths (the worktree, the home directory, the user name) with fixed placeholders, so logs do not reveal the machine (#156). A log that still fails the secret scan is held back. Logs are capped at 1 MiB.\n+- **Host mode and Docker.** A role without a `Dockerfile` runs as a host subprocess with your user's access, and ard logs `executor.host_untrusted`. That is acceptable on a machine you control with repositories you trust. A role with a `Dockerfile` runs in a container (#84). Use Docker for anything that reads untrusted code.\n+- **The bot-commit audit.** The bot bypasses branch protection on `main`, so a workflow audits main's first-parent history and fails if a bot commit changes anything but regular files under one `.ard/<issue#>/` directory.\n+- **The GitHub client does not follow redirects**, so a renamed or transferred repository is an error rather than a silent write elsewhere.\ndiff --git a/docs/site/troubleshooting.md b/docs/site/troubleshooting.md\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..731f9ef\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/docs/site/troubleshooting.md\n@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@\n+# Troubleshooting\n+\n+## Pushes are rejected with GH013\n+\n+The ruleset on `main` is rejecting ard's commit. Add the GitHub App to the ruleset's bypass list (see [Getting started](getting-started.md)).\n+\n+## ard complains that git is too old\n+\n+ard needs git 2.47.0 or newer. An older git fails some fetches with \"did not send all necessary objects\" (#165). Upgrade git and restart.\n+\n+## An issue has `ard:executor:failed`\n+\n+A job died or ran over time. Check the cause in the issue comment and the log under `.ard/<issue#>/logs/`, fix what it names (see [Operations](operations.md)) and remove the label to retry.\n+\n+## An issue has `ard:needs-human`\n+\n+An agent or a gate stopped. Read the last comment. Common reasons: no plan on `main`, a change over `diff_max_lines`, or a write outside the allowlist. Fix it, then remove the label.\n+\n+## An issue has `ard:tests:failed`\n+\n+The worker could not get `tests` green within `max_test_fixes` rounds. Read the pull request's test output, fix by hand or adjust the plan, and remove the label.\n+\n+## Nothing happens to my issue\n+\n+- With the default `label` intake mode, an issue must carry `ard:pending`.\n+- Check `ard config check` and that `paused` is not true.\n+- Check for `ard:hold` or `ard:pause`.\n+- With `require_approval` on, a worker waits for `ard:approved`.\n+- Run `ard serve --dry-run` and read the log lines.\n+\n+## The configuration is rejected\n+\n+`ard config check` names the file and key. Common causes are an unknown key, a duration written in days, or an `intake.label` that is one of ard's own labels.\n+\n+## Labels are missing or stale\n+\n+Run `ard labels sync`. It creates and updates labels and reports stale ones, but never deletes them.\ndiff --git a/go.mod b/go.mod\nindex f7eef86..149bda8 100644\n--- a/go.mod\n+++ b/go.mod\n@@ -23,7 +23,7 @@ require (\n \tgithub.com/mattn/go-isatty v0.0.24 // indirect\n \tgithub.com/ncruces/go-strftime v1.1.0 // indirect\n \tgithub.com/remyoudompheng/bigfft v0.0.0-20230129092748-24d4a6f8daec // indirect\n-\tgithub.com/spf13/pflag v1.0.9 // indirect\n+\tgithub.com/spf13/pflag v1.0.9\n \tgolang.org/x/mod v0.42.0 // indirect\n \tgolang.org/x/sync v0.24.0 // indirect\n \tgolang.org/x/sys v0.49.0 // indirect\ndiff --git a/internal/cli/commands.go b/internal/cli/commands.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..ab88ab7\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/internal/cli/commands.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@\n+package cli\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"sort\"\n+\n+\t\"github.com/spf13/cobra\"\n+\t\"github.com/spf13/pflag\"\n+)\n+\n+// CommandPaths lists every command path (\"ard config check\") with the\n+// names of the flags it accepts (\"--loops\"), including inherited global\n+// flags. The docs drift check reads it.\n+func CommandPaths() map[string][]string {\n+\tout := map[string][]string{}\n+\tvar walk func(c *cobra.Command)\n+\twalk = func(c *cobra.Command) {\n+\t\tvar flags []string\n+\t\tadd := func(f *pflag.Flag) { flags = append(flags, \"--\"+f.Name) }\n+\t\tc.LocalFlags().VisitAll(add)\n+\t\tc.InheritedFlags().VisitAll(add)\n+\t\tsort.Strings(flags)\n+\t\tout[c.CommandPath()] = flags\n+\t\tfor _, sub := range c.Commands() {\n+\t\t\twalk(sub)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\twalk(NewRoot(&App{}))\n+\treturn out\n+}\ndiff --git a/internal/cli/commands_test.go b/internal/cli/commands_test.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..24a8975\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/internal/cli/commands_test.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,18 @@\n+package cli\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"slices\"\n+\t\"testing\"\n+)\n+\n+func TestCommandPaths(t *testing.T) {\n+\tgot := CommandPaths()\n+\tfor _, p := range []string{\"ard\", \"ard serve\", \"ard init\", \"ard config check\", \"ard labels sync\", \"ard rebuild-state\", \"ard version\"} {\n+\t\tif _, ok := got[p]; !ok {\n+\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"missing command path %q\", p)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tif !slices.Contains(got[\"ard serve\"], \"--loops\") || !slices.Contains(got[\"ard serve\"], \"--dry-run\") {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"ard serve flags = %v\", got[\"ard serve\"])\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/internal/config/keys.go b/internal/config/keys.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..ba478b4\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/internal/config/keys.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@\n+package config\n+\n+// Keys returns every documented config key as a dotted path: the global\n+// keys in .ard/config.yaml and the per-role override keys. A map key is\n+// written as a placeholder (sidecars.<name>.repo). The user docs' key table\n+// is checked against this list, so a key added to the loader must be added\n+// here (TestKeysCoverRenderedConfig fails otherwise).\n+func Keys() []string {\n+\treturn []string{\n+\t\t\"max_pods\", \"executor\", \"agent\", \"require_approval\",\n+\t\t\"budget_usd_per_day\", \"max_budget_usd\", \"global_max_open\", \"paused\",\n+\t\t\"intervals.scanners\", \"intervals.triage\", \"intervals.workers\",\n+\t\t\"intervals.reviewers\", \"intervals.merger\", \"intervals.prwatch\",\n+\t\t\"pr_watch.pending_timeout\", \"roles_repo\",\n+\t\t\"intake.mode\", \"intake.label\",\n+\t\t\"merge.protected_paths\",\n+\t\t\"stale.inactive_for\", \"stale.suppress_for\",\n+\t\t\"redact.gitleaks.enabled\", \"redact.gitleaks.path\",\n+\t\t\"redact.gitleaks.block_autoclose_when_missing\",\n+\t\t\"sidecars.<name>.repo\", \"sidecars.<name>.ref\",\n+\t\t\"overrides\",\n+\t\t// Per-role override keys.\n+\t\t\"every\", \"max_open\", \"group_size\", \"autoclose\", \"route\",\n+\t\t\"model\", \"timeout\", \"sidecar\", \"after\", \"plan_section\",\n+\t\t\"tests\", \"max_test_fixes\", \"max_review_rounds\", \"reviewers\",\n+\t\t\"diff_warn_lines\", \"diff_max_lines\",\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/internal/config/keys_test.go b/internal/config/keys_test.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..45451aa\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/internal/config/keys_test.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,58 @@\n+package config\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"reflect\"\n+\t\"strings\"\n+\t\"testing\"\n+)\n+\n+// yamlPaths collects the dotted yaml-tag paths of leaf fields of t.\n+func yamlPaths(t reflect.Type, prefix string, out map[string]bool) {\n+\tfor i := 0; i < t.NumField(); i++ {\n+\t\ttag := strings.Split(t.Field(i).Tag.Get(\"yaml\"), \",\")[0]\n+\t\tif tag == \"\" || tag == \"-\" {\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tpath := prefix + tag\n+\t\tft := t.Field(i).Type\n+\t\tswitch {\n+\t\tcase ft.Kind() == reflect.Struct && ft.PkgPath() == t.PkgPath():\n+\t\t\tyamlPaths(ft, path+\".\", out)\n+\t\tcase ft.Kind() == reflect.Map:\n+\t\t\tyamlPaths(ft.Elem(), path+\".<name>.\", out)\n+\t\tdefault:\n+\t\t\tout[path] = true\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+func TestKeysCoverRenderedConfig(t *testing.T) {\n+\thave := map[string]bool{}\n+\tfor _, k := range Keys() {\n+\t\thave[k] = true\n+\t}\n+\tpaths := map[string]bool{}\n+\tyamlPaths(reflect.TypeOf(resolvedYAML{}), \"\", paths)\n+\tskip := map[string]bool{\"repo\": true, \"sha\": true, \"roles\": true}\n+\tfor p := range paths {\n+\t\tif skip[p] {\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif p == \"protected_paths\" {\n+\t\t\tp = \"merge.protected_paths\"\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif !have[p] {\n+\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"config key %q is rendered but missing from Keys()\", p)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\troles := map[string]bool{}\n+\tyamlPaths(reflect.TypeOf(roleYAML{}), \"\", roles)\n+\tfor _, k := range []string{\"agent\", \"model\", \"timeout\", \"sidecar\", \"every\", \"max_open\", \"group_size\", \"autoclose\", \"route\", \"after\", \"plan_section\", \"tests\", \"max_test_fixes\", \"max_review_rounds\", \"reviewers\", \"diff_warn_lines\", \"diff_max_lines\"} {\n+\t\tif !roles[k] {\n+\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"override key %q is no longer a roleYAML field\", k)\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif !have[k] && k != \"agent\" {\n+\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"override key %q missing from Keys()\", k)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/internal/docscheck/docscheck.go b/internal/docscheck/docscheck.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..68d43d0\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/internal/docscheck/docscheck.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,149 @@\n+// Package docscheck keeps the user docs under docs/site honest: every ard\n+// command and flag they name must exist in the CLI, and the configuration\n+// reference must list exactly the keys the config package knows.\n+package docscheck\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"fmt\"\n+\t\"io/fs\"\n+\t\"regexp\"\n+\t\"strings\"\n+)\n+\n+// IgnoreMarker on the line before a command skips the check for that line.\n+const IgnoreMarker = \"<!-- docscheck:ignore -->\"\n+\n+var (\n+\tinlineCode = regexp.MustCompile(\"`([^`\\n]+)`\")\n+\twordRe     = regexp.MustCompile(`^[a-z][a-z-]*$`)\n+\tkeyCell    = regexp.MustCompile(\"^\\\\|\\\\s*`([^`]+)`\")\n+)\n+\n+// CheckCommands scans every .md file in fsys. Commands in fenced blocks\n+// (a line starting with \"ard\", optionally after \"$ \") and in inline code\n+// spans starting with \"ard \" are checked against paths, a map of command\n+// path (\"ard serve\") to its accepted flags. It returns one problem per\n+// bad command.\n+func CheckCommands(fsys fs.FS, paths map[string][]string) ([]string, error) {\n+\tvar problems []string\n+\terr := fs.WalkDir(fsys, \".\", func(p string, d fs.DirEntry, err error) error {\n+\t\tif err != nil || d.IsDir() || !strings.HasSuffix(p, \".md\") {\n+\t\t\treturn err\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tdata, err := fs.ReadFile(fsys, p)\n+\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\treturn err\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tfenced, prev := false, \"\"\n+\t\tfor i, line := range strings.Split(string(data), \"\\n\") {\n+\t\t\ttrimmed := strings.TrimSpace(line)\n+\t\t\tignore := strings.TrimSpace(prev) == IgnoreMarker\n+\t\t\tprev = line\n+\t\t\tif strings.HasPrefix(trimmed, \"```\") {\n+\t\t\t\tfenced = !fenced\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tvar cmds []string\n+\t\t\tif fenced {\n+\t\t\t\tc := strings.TrimSpace(strings.TrimPrefix(trimmed, \"$ \"))\n+\t\t\t\tif c == \"ard\" || strings.HasPrefix(c, \"ard \") {\n+\t\t\t\t\tcmds = append(cmds, c)\n+\t\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\t} else {\n+\t\t\t\tfor _, m := range inlineCode.FindAllStringSubmatch(line, -1) {\n+\t\t\t\t\tif strings.HasPrefix(m[1], \"ard \") {\n+\t\t\t\t\t\tcmds = append(cmds, m[1])\n+\t\t\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tif ignore {\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tfor _, c := range cmds {\n+\t\t\t\tif msg := checkOne(c, paths); msg != \"\" {\n+\t\t\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, fmt.Sprintf(\"%s:%d: %s\", p, i+1, msg))\n+\t\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n+\t\treturn nil\n+\t})\n+\treturn problems, err\n+}\n+\n+func checkOne(cmd string, paths map[string][]string) string {\n+\tvar toks []string\n+\tfor _, t := range strings.Fields(cmd) {\n+\t\tif t == \"|\" || t == \"&&\" || t == \";\" || t == \">\" || strings.HasPrefix(t, \"#\") {\n+\t\t\tbreak\n+\t\t}\n+\t\ttoks = append(toks, t)\n+\t}\n+\tpath, i := \"ard\", 1\n+\tvar flagToks []string\n+\tfor ; i < len(toks); i++ {\n+\t\tif next := path + \" \" + toks[i]; !strings.HasPrefix(toks[i], \"-\") {\n+\t\t\tif _, ok := paths[next]; ok {\n+\t\t\t\tpath = next\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tbreak\n+\t}\n+\thasChildren := false\n+\tfor p := range paths {\n+\t\tif strings.HasPrefix(p, path+\" \") {\n+\t\t\thasChildren = true\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tif i < len(toks) && hasChildren && wordRe.MatchString(toks[i]) {\n+\t\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"unknown command %q\", path+\" \"+toks[i])\n+\t}\n+\tfor _, t := range toks[i:] {\n+\t\tif strings.HasPrefix(t, \"--\") {\n+\t\t\tflagToks = append(flagToks, t)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tfor _, f := range flagToks {\n+\t\tname, _, _ := strings.Cut(f, \"=\")\n+\t\tname = strings.TrimRight(name, \",.\")\n+\t\tfound := false\n+\t\tfor _, ok := range paths[path] {\n+\t\t\tif ok == name {\n+\t\t\t\tfound = true\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tif !found {\n+\t\t\treturn fmt.Sprintf(\"%q has no flag %s\", path, name)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\treturn \"\"\n+}\n+\n+// CheckKeys compares the backticked keys in the first column of the\n+// tables in the configuration reference (page, an fs path) with keys.\n+func CheckKeys(fsys fs.FS, page string, keys []string) ([]string, error) {\n+\tdata, err := fs.ReadFile(fsys, page)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn nil, err\n+\t}\n+\tdocumented := map[string]bool{}\n+\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(string(data), \"\\n\") {\n+\t\tif m := keyCell.FindStringSubmatch(line); m != nil {\n+\t\t\tdocumented[m[1]] = true\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\twant := map[string]bool{}\n+\tvar problems []string\n+\tfor _, k := range keys {\n+\t\twant[k] = true\n+\t\tif !documented[k] {\n+\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, fmt.Sprintf(\"%s: config key %q is not documented\", page, k))\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tfor k := range documented {\n+\t\tif !want[k] {\n+\t\t\tproblems = append(problems, fmt.Sprintf(\"%s: documented key %q does not exist\", page, k))\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\treturn problems, nil\n+}\ndiff --git a/internal/docscheck/docscheck_test.go b/internal/docscheck/docscheck_test.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..a92c581\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/internal/docscheck/docscheck_test.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,94 @@\n+package docscheck\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"os\"\n+\t\"path/filepath\"\n+\t\"sort\"\n+\t\"strings\"\n+\t\"testing\"\n+\t\"testing/fstest\"\n+\n+\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/cli\"\n+\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/config\"\n+)\n+\n+func one(body string) fstest.MapFS {\n+\treturn fstest.MapFS{\"a.md\": {Data: []byte(body)}}\n+}\n+\n+func TestCheckCommandsFixtures(t *testing.T) {\n+\tpaths := cli.CommandPaths()\n+\tcases := []struct {\n+\t\tname, body, want string\n+\t}{\n+\t\t{\"unknown command\", \"Run `ard bogus`.\", `unknown command \"ard bogus\"`},\n+\t\t{\"unknown flag\", \"Run `ard serve --nope`.\", \"no flag --nope\"},\n+\t\t{\"known flag\", \"Run `ard serve --dry-run`.\", \"\"},\n+\t\t{\"global flag on subcommand\", \"`ard config check --repo a/b --ref main`\", \"\"},\n+\t\t{\"fenced prompt\", \"```sh\\n$ ard labels sync --dry-run\\n```\", \"\"},\n+\t\t{\"fenced bad\", \"```sh\\nard labels nope\\n```\", \"unknown command\"},\n+\t\t{\"ignored\", \"<!-- docscheck:ignore -->\\nRun `ard bogus`.\", \"\"},\n+\t\t{\"prose only\", \"ard is a tool. `ard:pending` and `ard-bot`.\", \"\"},\n+\t\t{\"placeholder arg\", \"`ard rebuild-state <scanner>`\", \"\"},\n+\t}\n+\tfor _, c := range cases {\n+\t\tgot, err := CheckCommands(one(c.body), paths)\n+\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tjoined := strings.Join(got, \"\\n\")\n+\t\tif c.want == \"\" && joined != \"\" || !strings.Contains(joined, c.want) {\n+\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"%s: problems = %q, want %q\", c.name, joined, c.want)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+func TestCheckKeysFixtures(t *testing.T) {\n+\tkeys := []string{\"max_pods\", \"paused\"}\n+\tgot, _ := CheckKeys(one(\"| `max_pods` | 3 |\\n| `old_key` | x |\\n\"), \"a.md\", keys)\n+\tsort.Strings(got)\n+\tjoined := strings.Join(got, \"\\n\")\n+\tif !strings.Contains(joined, `\"paused\" is not documented`) || !strings.Contains(joined, `\"old_key\" does not exist`) {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"problems = %q\", joined)\n+\t}\n+\tif got, _ := CheckKeys(one(\"| `max_pods` |\\n| `paused` |\\n\"), \"a.md\", keys); len(got) != 0 {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"unexpected problems %q\", got)\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+func siteDir(t *testing.T) string {\n+\tdir, err := os.Getwd()\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\tfor {\n+\t\tif _, err := os.Stat(filepath.Join(dir, \"go.mod\")); err == nil {\n+\t\t\treturn filepath.Join(dir, \"docs\", \"site\")\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tparent := filepath.Dir(dir)\n+\t\tif parent == dir {\n+\t\t\tt.Fatal(\"go.mod not found\")\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tdir = parent\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+func TestRealDocsCommandsExist(t *testing.T) {\n+\tproblems, err := CheckCommands(os.DirFS(siteDir(t)), cli.CommandPaths())\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\tfor _, p := range problems {\n+\t\tt.Error(p)\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+func TestRealDocsConfigKeysMatch(t *testing.T) {\n+\tproblems, err := CheckKeys(os.DirFS(siteDir(t)), \"configuration.md\", config.Keys())\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\tfor _, p := range problems {\n+\t\tt.Error(p)\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/mkdocs.yml b/mkdocs.yml\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..2302c71\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/mkdocs.yml\n@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@\n+site_name: ard\n+site_description: Automated repository development\n+site_url: https://albertcbrown.github.io/ard/\n+repo_url: https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard\n+docs_dir: docs/site